# Modules the kernel does not load on demand (mu300-modprobe-guard; FINDINGS 163). One module name per line, as the
# kernel writes it (- as _). The guard refuses a request when it names one of these or matches one of their aliases
# (the alias= strings of its file), however it is spelled: net-pf-29, can, a genetlink family, an rtnl-link kind, fs-hfs...
# Every module that depends on one of these is on the list too (make-bundle.sh checks it against the build's modules.dep), so
# no request reaches them through a dependency either.
# Root loads any of them deliberately with `modprobe NAME` (that runs /sbin/modprobe, not the guard), and
# `touch /etc/mu300/autoload-allow-all` turns the guard off. The protocol families with no use here at all (RDS,
# ATM, AX.25, X.25, AppleTalk, Phonet, RxRPC/AFS, KCM, SMC, CAIF, ISDN, QRTR, DCCP, n_gsm, n_hdlc) are not built.
# Kept loadable on purpose: l2tp_* (L2TP VPNs, xl2tpd on OpenWrt). The VMs' vsock (vhost_vsock) is on the list: root
# loads it with `modprobe vhost_vsock` before starting a VM that wants it.
#
# TIPC
tipc
tipc_diag
# SCTP
sctp
sctp_diag
# CAN (AF_CAN; the USB CAN adapters' drivers need only can_dev, which stays)
can
can_raw
can_bcm
